Ruby and Java are both object-oriented languages with different design goals. In 2026: 1) Ruby - dynamic, interpreted, 2) Java - static, compiled, 3) Ruby - elegant syntax, 4) Java - verbose syntax, 5) Ruby - dynamic typing, 6) Java - static typing, 7) Ruby - Rails, 8) Java - Spring, 9) Ruby - faster development, 10) Java - better performance. Choose Ruby for rapid development, Java for performance and enterprise.

Example:
```ruby
Ruby vs Java:

    1. Ruby Example
       class User
           attr_accessor :name, :age

           def initialize(name, age)
               @name = name
               @age = age
           end

           def greet
               \"Hello, I'm #{@name}\"
           end
       end

       user = User.new('John', 30)
       puts user.greet

    2. Java Example
       public class User {
           private String name;
           private int age;

           public User(String name, int age) {
               this.name = name;
               this.age = age;
           }

           public String getName() {
               return name;
           }

           public int getAge() {
               return age;
           }

           public String greet() {
               return \"Hello, I'm \" + name;
           }
       }

       public class Main {
           public static void main(String[] args) {
               User user = new User(\"John\", 30);
               System.out.println(user.greet());
           }
       }

    3. Comparison Table
       Feature | Ruby | Java
       --------|------|-------
       Typing | Dynamic | Static
       Execution | Interpreted | Compiled
       Syntax | Elegant | Verbose
       Framework | Rails | Spring
       Performance | Slower | Faster
       Enterprise | Limited | Excellent
       Learning | Easier | Harder
       Use Case | Startups | Enterprise

    4. When to Choose
       Ruby | Java
       ----|--------
       Rapid development | Enterprise
       Startups | Large companies
       Rails | Spring
       Elegant code | Type safety
       MVP | Long-term projects
```

Detailed Explanation:
Ruby is dynamic with elegant syntax for rapid development (Rails). Java is static with verbose syntax for enterprise and performance (Spring). Choose Ruby for speed, Java for enterprise.

Ruby and TypeScript are both modern languages with different use cases. In 2026: 1) Ruby - dynamic, interpreted, 2) TypeScript - static, transpiled, 3) Ruby - Rails/web, 4) TypeScript - Angular/React, 5) Ruby - elegant syntax, 6) TypeScript - JavaScript superset, 7) Ruby - server-side, 8) TypeScript - client-side, 9) Ruby - smaller community, 10) TypeScript - larger community. Choose Ruby for backend, TypeScript for frontend.

Example:
```ruby
Ruby vs TypeScript:

    1. Ruby Example
       class User
           attr_accessor :name, :age

           def initialize(name, age)
               @name = name
               @age = age
           end
       end

       user = User.new('John', 30)
       puts user.name

    2. TypeScript Example
       class User {
           name: string;
           age: number;

           constructor(name: string, age: number) {
               this.name = name;
               this.age = age;
           }
       }

       const user = new User('John', 30);
       console.log(user.name);

    3. Comparison Table
       Feature | Ruby | TypeScript
       --------|------|-----------
       Typing | Dynamic | Static
       Platform | Server | Browser
       Framework | Rails | Angular/React
       Syntax | Elegant | JavaScript
       Use Case | Backend | Frontend

    4. When to Choose
       Ruby | TypeScript
       ----|-----------
       Backend APIs | Frontend
       Rails | Angular/React
       Server-side | Client-side
```

Detailed Explanation:
Ruby is dynamic with Rails for server-side development. TypeScript is static with Angular/React for client-side development. Choose Ruby for backend, TypeScript for frontend.

